---
import ProsePage from '../../templates/ProsePage.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes.ts';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../config/site.ts';
---

<ProsePage
  locale="en"
  routeKey="partners"
  title="Partners"
  description="How organisations support twente.dev — communities, sponsors and hosts — and the safeguards that money can never buy past."
>
  <p>
    twente.dev prices access to outcomes, not to the audience. Partners fund food, venues and
    production; in return they get visible credit, places for their people, and aggregated evidence
    that the evening worked. What no partner gets, at any amount: attendee data, stage time,
    exclusivity, or a say in editorial decisions.
  </p>

  <h2>Communities and meetups: always free</h2>
  <p>
    Every technology community in the region gets a free directory profile, a listing in the shared
    calendar and a co-promotion slot — and keeps its own identity, its own member list and its own
    stage. We never import member lists, we never rebrand someone else's event as ours, and every
    listing links to the source. Listing an event costs nothing and never will.
    <a href={routePath('contribute', 'en')}>List your event or community →</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Partner types</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt><strong>Founding partner</strong></dt>
    <dd>
      Launch-year support through March 2027, capped at four, no sector exclusivity. Founding
      credit, six places per edition, one editorially reviewed practitioner story, an advisory
      briefing and the aggregated impact report.
    </dd>
    <dt><strong>Event partner</strong></dt>
    <dd>
      Per-edition support, capped at four for edition 001. Event credit, four places, one approved
      employee contribution and a post-event results snapshot.
    </dd>
    <dt><strong>Community supporter</strong></dt>
    <dd>Two places, credit on this page, inclusion in the results report.</dd>
    <dt><strong>Host partner</strong></dt>
    <dd>
      Venue or facilities in kind, valued at real avoided cost, with clear host credit and four
      guest places.
    </dd>
    <dt><strong>Network partner</strong></dt>
    <dd>
      No fee, no commercial benefits, no invoice — mutual distribution and coordination between
      organisations that want the same healthy ecosystem.
    </dd>
  </dl>
  <p>
    Contribution levels are agreed per edition after a short discovery call — one recommendation, no
    menu of options. Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> for the current partner
    brief.
  </p>

  <h2>The safeguards</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No attendee data.</strong> Partners receive aggregated counts only — never names, never
      contact details, never "just the ones who consented".
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No paid speaking.</strong> Speakers are selected editorially. A partner's employee can be
      on stage for the same reason anyone else can: a story worth telling.
    </li>
    <li><strong>No exclusivity.</strong> No sector locks, no "sole partner" claims.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>No approval rights.</strong> Partners see the programme when the community does.
    </li>
    <li><strong>No dependence.</strong> No single source provides more than 30% of annual cash.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Acceptable alternatives when a request crosses a line: employee participation, an editorially
    reviewed field report, aggregated insight, or a host activation.
  </p>

  <h2>Transparency</h2>
  <p>
    We publish the partner list with the type of contribution — cash, in-kind or network — clearly
    distinguished, an annual results summary, and a public report after every flagship event,
    including what did not work.
  </p>

  <h2>Current partners</h2>
  <p>
    Partner conversations for twente.dev/001 are underway; confirmed partners appear here with their
    contribution type. Being early has its advantages —
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>talk to us</a>.
  </p>
</ProsePage>
